WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.698
Security-relevant file events should be observable.

2
00:00:03.698 --> 00:00:15.435
Useful logs often include uploads, downloads, deletes, shares, permission changes, scan results, processing failures, retention actions, and unusual access patterns.

3
00:00:15.435 --> 00:00:23.233
Logs should identify the actor, tenant, file identifier, event type, result, timestamp, and safe error category.

4
00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:30.468
They should not include sensitive file contents, secrets, full private document text, or raw binary data.

5
00:00:30.468 --> 00:00:35.693
Audit trails help answer operational questions after a mistake or incident.

6
00:00:35.693 --> 00:00:37.221
Who uploaded the file.

7
00:00:37.221 --> 00:00:38.507
Who downloaded it.

8
00:00:38.507 --> 00:00:39.472
Was it shared.

9
00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.160
Did permissions change.

10
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.375
Was a file deleted before its retention period.

11
00:00:44.375 --> 00:00:46.626
Which processing job touched it.

12
00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:56.755
These questions are difficult to answer if file events are logged only as generic web requests or if storage actions bypass the application entirely.

13
00:00:56.755 --> 00:01:00.212
Monitoring should look for unusual file activity.

14
00:01:00.212 --> 00:01:15.004
Examples include sudden upload spikes, repeated failed validations, large export volumes, downloads from unusual locations, sharing changes on sensitive files, scanner failures, and storage permission changes.

15
00:01:15.004 --> 00:01:24.651
Alerting should be tuned to the workflow and data classification so teams see meaningful risk signals rather than a flood of routine activity.

16
00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:30.519
Retention, deletion, backup, and incident response are part of secure file handling.

17
00:01:30.519 --> 00:01:39.684
Files should not remain forever by accident, and backups should be protected according to the sensitivity of the content they contain.

18
00:01:39.684 --> 00:01:52.707
If a file is exposed, teams need to know who accessed it, whether links or signed URLs must be revoked, whether downstream copies exist, and what notification or remediation obligations apply.
